2026-11-07T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2482) 'Does the reader bend survive a realistic table?
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2482  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2482) 'In the pair matrix, per-pair reads fell to 0.65-0.67 of M=1 at M=8 with the writer fixed at 50 commits/s, and to 0.47-0.48 with the writer at max rate. The current test table has 2,000 rows, about 500 KB, with a total store size probably around 4 MB, which data distribution almost certainly keeps in one shard on one storage process; adding storage processes would then change nothing, so a "not the cluster" conclusion drawn from it would be wrong. Under NORMAL locking, SQLite drops its whole page cache on every head move, so each uncached page costs one round trip when the speculative get hits and two when it misses, and a 500 KB table flatters that cost. Three candidate causes must stay separate: one storage process serving every page read, the VFS re-fetching pages after each head move, and this Mac.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2482  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2482) 'A measured partitions_count above one (or a stated reason it stayed at one), and the M=8 reader ratio on the feed-shaped table with its confounds stated.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2482  1 LINE
 Text:Written

